{-
   The one kernel call the allocator needs - `lib/Native/Linux.x64.yana`'s `mapMemory`, cut down to
   what `compiler/resolve_mir` compiles.

   `Linux.x64.yana` and not a plain name: the selectors in a file name are what decide whether a file
   is part of this module at all (Analysis-Modules.md §2.5), so a build for another kernel simply does
   not read this and the heap below it finds no `mapMemory` to call. The real library's file is where
   the whole call table is; nothing here is a second opinion about any of it.

   **The numbers are written out.** `lib/` reads them off a `SysCall` enum with `@value`, which is the
   right shape and needs a `deriving`-style numbering this stage has not got. Each one is named in a
   comment instead, which is the same information in the place a reader of this file will look.
-}

-- `PROT_READ | PROT_WRITE`, and `MAP_PRIVATE | MAP_ANONYMOUS` - storage of our own that no file backs.
let protReadWrite = 3 :: USize
let mapPrivateAnonymous = 34 :: USize

{-
   `mmap` is call number 9 on x86-64.

   The descriptor is -1 as a **word** rather than as a number: every argument position is a word, and
   this is the one place the kernel's "no file" is a bit pattern instead of a count.

   A negative answer is the negated errno, which is the kernel's own convention and is where this
   file's knowledge stops - the null is what `allocateHeap` reads, and turning a number into an error
   value is nothing a heap needs.
-}
pub fn mapMemory(size: Size) -> %U8:
    let anonymous = bitcast(-1 :: Size) :: USize
    let result = syscall6(9, 0, bitcast(size) :: USize, protReadWrite, mapPrivateAnonymous,
                          anonymous, 0)

    if result < 0 then return null()
    return bitcast(result) :: %U8
